<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Pandosia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/331-bce">331 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Pandosia was fought in 331 BCE near the town of Pandosia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruttium">Bruttium</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander I of Epirus</a>, uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, who had come to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> against the Italic peoples, was defeated and killed there by the Lucanians and Bruttians. His death ended his campaign of conquest in southern Italy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander I of Epirus</a> arrived in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with his army in 334 or 333 BCE, hoping to match in the west the conquests of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in the east. A call for help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>, then at war with the Bruttians, gave him the occasion. Ancient historians say the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona</a> had warned him to beware the river Acheron and the city of Pandosia, which he took to mean the river and city of those names in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>.</p><p>He defeated the Bruttians and Lucanians and captured several cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> names Heraclea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipontum">Sipontum</a>, Cosentia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terina">Terina</a>, and says he sent three hundred noble families to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> as hostages; Justin records alliances with Metapontum, the Peucetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> grew strained, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> writes that he tried to move a panhellenic festival from Heraclea to Thurii out of enmity with the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 331 BCE Alexander camped near Pandosia, on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruttium">Bruttium</a>, on three hills a short distance apart, accompanied by two hundred Lucanian exiles. Continuous rain flooded the fields and cut the hills off from each other, and the Lucanians and Bruttians attacked, destroying the two parts of the army separated from the king and blockading his own hill. The exiles offered to hand Alexander over, dead or alive, in return for their property.</p><p>Alexander broke out with a small group, killing the Lucanian general. At a river ford he learned that the stream was called the Acheron and hesitated, remembering the oracle, but with his pursuers approaching he rode into the water, where a Lucanian exile killed him with a javelin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to Justin, the city of Thurii ransomed his body and buried it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says the victors cut it in half, until a woman whose husband and children were hostages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> had the remains cremated at Cosentia and the bones sent home to Alexander's wife Cleopatra and his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>. The battle is seen as marking the end of Greek colonisation in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and a rare defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> by lighter Italian troops fighting in small companies on hilly ground.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Pandosia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They defeated Thurii near Laus in 389 BCE and killed Alexander of Epirus at <b>Pandosia</b> in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a></li></ul></section>
